    "body": "Union Pacific reported record third-quarter revenue and profits as higher volume , pricing, and efficiency gains more than offset the impact of rising fuel prices and inflation.\n“Union Pacific’s third-quarter results demonstrate our ability to safely deliver the service we sold our customers while efficiently handling 6% volume growth,” Chief Executive Jim Vena said on the railroad’s (NYSE: UNP ) earnings call today.\nOperating income increased 15%, to $2.94 billion, as revenue grew 15%, to $7.19 billion. Earnings per share rose 16%, to $3.49.\nThe railroad’s operating ratio was 59.1%, a 0.1-point improvement compared to a year ago. Workforce productivity, measured by car-miles per employee, improved 5%.\nOverall volume was up 6% for the quarter. Premium volume — which includes intermodal and automotive traffic — was up 9%. Industrial products traffic grew 6% in the quarter, while bulk volume was flat.\n“Domestic intermodal delivered its fifth consecutive record quarter in both volume and revenue. Our foundation of service combined with strong business development and the deployment of buffer resources enabled 13% volume growth,” said Kenny Rocker, executive vice president of marketing and sales. “International intermodal volumes improved 8% versus last year driven by strong West Coast import volumes and business wins.”\nThe carrier offered a positive outlook for fourth-quarter volume, as high fuel prices and tight truck capacity will continue to drive conversions of truckload freight to rail. The lone exception is coal volume, which will remain under pressure due to low natural gas prices and high utility stockpiles.\nRocker expects a third straight year of record domestic intermodal volume.\nOverall volume growth prompted UP to shift some capital spending planned for next year into this year. UP now expects to spend $3.5 billion on capital projects this year, up from $3.3 billion.\nUP’s key operational metrics improved slightly compared to last year, with car miles per day up 1%, average train speed flat, and terminal dwell down by 2%. Train length grew 2%, to an average of 9,969 feet.\n“Operating discipline and consistent execution of the fundamentals drove another quarter of record workforce productivity, record train length, and record fuel consumption,” said Eric Gehringer, executive vice president of operations.\nThe railroad said its employee personal injury and train accident rates improved compared to the three-year rolling average but did not provide specifics.\nSubscribe to FreightWaves’ Rail e-newsletter and get the latest insights on rail freight right in your inbox.\nRead more:\nNorfolk Southern profits rise on higher volume and revenue\nCommodity markets whip up railfreight gains\nFirst look: Union Pacific earnings\nFirst look: Norfolk Southern earnings\nCSX profit jumps on volume and revenue growth\nUpcoming FreightWaves Events\nCompliance\nBrokerage Compliance Symposium\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nAwards\nF3 Awards Dinner\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nFreightTech\nF3: Future of Freight Festival\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nOctober 27, 2026 – October 28, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nCompliance Brokerage Compliance Symposium Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nAwards F3 Awards Dinner Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nFreightTech F3: Future of Freight Festival Oct 27 – Oct 28 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now",
